course: dictionary
lesson: 9160
title: Snow: a noun and a verb
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: The word snow can be a noun or a verb. As a noun, it names a thing: «The snow was real.» As a verb, it names an action: «It's snowing!» The verb changes its form: snows, snowed, snowing. Notice which form fits the sentence.
explain: Use the clues around the word to decide. A noun often comes after words like the or a. A verb tells you what happens, so in «Later that day, it snowed», the action is that snow fell.
explain: Sometimes the meaning is not about weather. «Snowed under» means very busy, so a person snowed under with homework has a lot to do. Always ask whether the sentence is describing the weather or something else.
explain: Snow can also be dangerous. «A boy in Alaska tells his friend not to rub snow on her frozen cheeks.» Snow and ice are slippery, so wear warm clothes and do not walk on frozen ponds.
fact: Snow is soft white flakes of frozen water that fall from clouds when it is very cold.
fact: Snow forms when water vapour freezes in cold clouds, and every snowflake has six sides.
fact: Snow melts at 0 °C into water.
fact: Snowman, snowball, snowflake and snow day are compound words built from snow.
fact: In a fronted adverbial such as «Later that day, it snowed», the phrase comes at the start and is followed by a comma.
exercise: In «The snow was real», what kind of word is snow? | *Noun | Verb | Adjective | Adverb
exercise: In «It's snowing!», what kind of word is snowing? | *Verb | Noun | Adjective | Preposition
exercise: Find the fronted adverbial in «Later that day, it snowed.» | it snowed | *Later that day | day | snowed
test: What does «snowed under» mean? | Very cold | Frozen solid | Covered in snow | *Very busy
test: At what temperature does snow melt into water? | 100 °C | 10 °C | *0 °C | -10 °C
exam: Which is the safest choice on a frozen pond? | Throw snowballs across it | *Stay off it and keep away from the ice | Skate on it with friends | Walk across it to see if it holds
